Thanks, Vince.  It is good to know about the lag() function.

In this case, there are sometimes repeated sleep recordings by the same person on the same date (such as the nap in row 6 of nights.all), so I would like to sum those.  There are also many dates when sleep was not recorded, so I will probably need to retrieve the sleep data by date, rather than using lag() (unless there is some way of getting lag to understand that I don't want anything in cases where the previous date does not exist for that person).

Also, I should have been more clear that I would like to get the data on prior sleep from the nights.all data frame, because sleep obtained on the day before an observation would still be relevant even if the day before did not, itself, meet criteria for inclusion in the analysis.  So, part of what I am trying to figure out is a way to get dplyr to retrieve data from a different data frame while staying within the grouping by person.id <http://person.id> in the data frame it was given.  I tried the following as a quick test of that idea, but got an error, so I guess dplyr doesn't work the way I am trying to use it.

> group_by(nights, person.id <http://person.id>) %>%
+   summarize(n.all=length(which(nights.all$person.id <http://person.id>==person.id <http://person.id>)))
Error in which(c(1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 5) == person.id <http://person.id>) :
  object 'person.id <http://person.id>' not found

Bonnie

